{
  "description": "Reference dependence measures for the same 95 documents (11 with a declared base, 18 that cite a source held in sdo-sources). pointer_rate = pointer phrases ('as defined in', 'see', 'according to' ...) followed within 10 words by a standards body or outside document identifier, per 1,000 words, contents lines excluded. Only pointer_rate is classed, separately for documents with and without a declared base. copied_per_1000 = words per 1,000 of the document's words in stretches of 12+ words shared word for word with an outside source in sdo-sources, with TM Forum's section 14.1 notice masked out; it is classed only against the documents that cite a held source (cites_held_source), since for the rest a zero only means their sources weren't held. Heavy also needs at least min_heavy_pointers pointers, because most documents without a declared base have none and one or two in a short document would otherwise class as heavy.",
  "sigma_multiplier": 0.8,
  "min_heavy_pointers": 3,
  "copied_class_rule": {
    "light_below": 1,
    "heavy_above": 20,
    "note": "Fixed rule: light below 1, typical 1 to 20, heavy above 20 copied words per 1,000 words. Mean - 0.8 sigma (mean 9.0, sigma 15.2) is below zero (-3.2), so classing at mean +/- 0.8 sigma could never give light."
  },
  "pointer_light_at_zero": [
    "none"
  ],
  "pointer_light_note": "For documents without a declared base, light means no pointers at all: 70 of the 84 have none, so mean - 0.8 sigma is below zero (-0.07) and could never give light.",
